---
/*
  about.astro — the longer first person piece.

  The opening four paragraphs are Jack's own writing, carried over from the old
  homepage About section. The only change is to the last one, where a colon was
  restructured away to match the site's punctuation rule. Everything after that
  is drafted from the résumé and the fact pack.
*/
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="John Jacobson is an electrical engineering student at the University of Illinois, working toward embedded systems and firmware."
>
  <PageHeader title="About" />

  <div class="container body">
    <div class="intro">
      <div class="intro__portrait">
        <Figure slot="headshot" loading="eager" />
      </div>

      <div class="prose intro__text">
        <p>
          I'm John Jacobson, though most people call me Jack. I'm an engineering
          student in the Grainger College of Engineering at the University of
          Illinois, focused on electrical engineering.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I've always chased curiosity, the need to figure out how things work,
        but the one thing I could never quite understand was electricity. Tiny,
        invisible electrons that light up the world, power our computers, and
        drive everything around us. Trying to understand it led me to build my
        own computer in middle school, and computers for my friends. It led me
        to my first CS class, to Arduinos, and to studying the physics of
        electricity and magnetism.
      </p>

      <p>
        What I love about electrical engineering is its almost magical nature.
        It lets you build what should be impossible, solving physical and
        software limitations at once to arrive at something compact and elegant
        that makes life better. To me, a device that can hold and display human
        knowledge is genuinely otherworldly.
      </p>

      <h2>What I am studying</h2>
      <p>
        I started at Illinois in August 2025 and expect to finish in 2029. The
        coursework that has mattered most so far is ECE 120 and ECE 220, which
        took me from logic gates and Karnaugh maps through C and LC-3 assembly,
        alongside ECE 110, electromagnetism in PHYS 212, differential equations,
        and discrete mathematics.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most of what I know about C++ has come from building things outside of
        class and working through learncpp.com, rather than from a course.
      </p>

      <h2>What I want to build</h2>
      <p>
        I want to keep chasing that curiosity into building the kind of products
        I grew up admiring, the ones that are elegant and compact and woven so
        deeply into daily life that people forget how much engineering sits
        behind them.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice that means embedded systems and firmware, or FPGA and ASIC
        design. I am looking for an internship in that direction.
      </p>

      <h2>Outside of class</h2>
      <p>
        I earned Eagle Scout in December 2024. My service project built planter
        benches with built in catnip planters for the Forsyth County Humane
        Society, which meant sourcing materials, managing a budget, and
        directing a team of more than thirty volunteers. I also served as my
        troop's Senior Patrol Leader.
      </p>
      <p>
        At Illinois I am rush chair for Phi Gamma Delta, where I run recruitment
        and organise the events around it. I am also a member of the IEEE
        student chapter and SIGARCH, and the workshops there on soldering,
        circuit design, and ASIC design have been some of the more useful hours
        I have spent outside a classroom.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="aside">
      <Figure
        slot="eagle-benches"
        caption="The Eagle service project, built for the Forsyth County Humane Society."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Away from all of that I run Arch Linux with Hyprland because I enjoy the
        tinkering more than I need the desktop to be finished. I write C++ for
        fun, including a Pong clone in raylib, and I model things in Blender and
        Onshape. I hike, I travel when I get the chance, and I have a four year
        student season ticket for Illinois football.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .body {
    padding-block: var(--space-5) var(--space-6);
  }

  .intro {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .intro__portrait {
    max-width: 200px;
  }

  /* Side by side once there is room for the portrait and a readable column. */
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .intro {
      grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-5);
      align-items: start;
    }
  }

  .aside {
    max-width: 560px;
    margin-block: var(--space-5);
  }
</style>
